In this lesson we'll look at why scientists use logarithmic scales to measure things that vary over enormous ranges. Sounds, acidity, and earthquakes can differ by factors of millions, so a normal linear chart would be impossibly tall. The trick is to plot the logarithm of the value instead of the value itself, which means equal steps along the axis represent equal ratios, not equal differences. That single idea unlocks three famous scales you'll meet again and again. Decibels measure sound intensity using ten times the log of the intensity ratio, so a ten-decibel jump means the sound is actually ten times louder. pH measures acidity by taking the negative log of the hydrogen ion concentration, so dropping the pH by one means ten times more acid. And the Richter scale for earthquakes works the same way at heart — a one-point rise in magnitude means ten times the ground shaking amplitude. Once you've used one log scale, the others feel familiar. Now let's see it in action.